Upstream availability
---------------------
The upstream repository was deleted from GitHub and now returns 404. The
pinned commit above was recovered from the network mirror
https://github.com/kodjodevf/M-Extension-Server by SHA
(`git fetch --depth=1 <mirror> ee55c65106bb18bf81a5ddc660d321b4e14ea2f9`), and
its LICENSE is byte-identical to the LICENSE already vendored here
(sha256 3f3d9e0024b1921b067d6f7f88deb4a60cbe7a78e76c64e3f1d7fc3b779b9d04).

Because no reachable upstream remains, the source is vendored in this
directory instead of being cloned at build time.

Layout
------
upstream_src/               pristine upstream tree at the pinned commit,
                            byte-for-byte, no Hibiki edits.
overlay/                    Hibiki-modified source files; copied over
                            upstream_src after the patch is applied.
server-build.gradle.patch   Hibiki changes to upstream files that are too
                            small to justify a full-file overlay.
LICENSE / NOTICE            MPL-2.0 text and the modification notice.

upstream_src/ excludes exactly two things from the upstream commit:
  .git/       clone metadata, not source
  .DS_Store   macOS Finder metadata that upstream had committed
Everything else is vendored verbatim, including the Gradle wrapper JAR, the
AndroidCompat Noto font resources (loaded by the static initialiser in
AndroidCompat/src/main/java/android/graphics/Typeface.java, so dropping them
would change the shipped JAR's behaviour) and upstream's own .gitattributes
(same EOL policy as Hibiki's, so no normalisation drift).

Build
-----
tool/mihon/build_desktop_runtime.sh (macOS) and
tool/mihon/build_desktop_runtime.ps1 (Windows) do, in this order:
  1. copy upstream_src/ into a temporary directory;
  2. `git apply server-build.gradle.patch` (git apply works outside a git
     repository);
  3. copy overlay/ over the result, overwriting same-named files;
  4. run `:server:test :server:shadowJar` with the pinned Temurin JDK.
The overlay is applied last, so the security boundary below always wins.

server-build.gradle.patch MUST keep its default 3 lines of context and MUST
NOT be applied with `--unidiff-zero` (BUG-1428). A zero-context patch has
nothing to verify in an insertion-only hunk: `git apply --check` returns 0
even after upstream_src has drifted, and the real apply then blind-inserts by
line number (measured: the JGroupFilter `stateString` field landed between
`name` and `type` — a Kotlin data class's field order is positional, so it
still compiles while already behaving wrong). With context, a pure line shift
is relocated automatically and a real content change fails loudly.
Regenerate the patch with `git diff` (never `git diff -U0`).

upstream server/build.gradle.kts derives its revision string from
`git rev-list HEAD --count`, which yields nothing in a .git-less vendored
tree. Both scripts therefore set upstream's own `ProductRevision` escape
hatch to the pinned commit's short SHA (ee55c65), so the produced JAR name
and manifest point straight at the commit recorded above.

